lack\'s line of 4 diagonal stones is a clear winning chance. The coordinates are right. Black can win. I must block D4. There\'s no other way. This isn\'t just a threat. It\'s a game ender if I don\'t respond.\n\nLet\'s double-check the co-ordinates. D4 is not listed as occupied. It is open.\n\nTherefore, the move is D4. I must place my stone there, to prevent Black from winning.\n\nEvaluation: The opponent (Black) has built a strong diagonal chain consisting of stones at E5, F6, G7, and H8. This forms a "four-in-a-row". Upon checking the ends of this chain, the position I9 is blocked by a White stone, but the position D4 is empty. This constitutes an immediate "threat to win" (Mate in 1); if Black plays D4, they get 5 connected stones. White has no immediate winning moves (like a four-in-a-row of their own) to counter-attack instantly.\n\nReason: To prevent Black from winning on the next turn with a 5-in-a-row diagonal connection (D4-E5-F6-G7-H8), White is forced to block the open end of Black\'s chain at D4.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "D", "row": 4}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state reveals a critical threat from the opponent (Black). Black has established a continuous diagonal line of four stones located at E5, F6, G7, and H8. I analyzed the ends of this sequence:\n- The bottom-right extension point at I9 is blocked by a White stone.\n- The top-left extension point at D4 is currently empty.\nThis formation represents an immediate winning threat (often called a "sleeping four" or "four"). If it is Black\'s turn to play there, they will complete a 5-in-a-row. Since White does not have any existing 4-in-a-row or "open 3" patterns that could lead to an immediate counter-win, White must play defensively.\n\nReason: Black threatens to win immediately by playing at D4, which would complete a diagonal line of five stones (D4, E5, F6, G7, H8). This move is a forced block to prolong the game and stop the opponent from winning.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "D", "row": 4}'}
